tures · cite101†Quick start · cite102†Documentation L217: \nL218: * * *\nL219: \nL220: ServingStudio is an integrated workbench for analyzing, simulating, and optimizing LLM serving systems. ServingStudio consists of three components:\nL221:   * ServingStudio Sim predicts serving system performance using measured GPU kernel timings.\nL222:   * ServingStudio Agent drives the simulation, investigates results, and helps implement and validate improvements in real serving frameworks.\nL223:   * ServingStudio UI provides results visualization and zero-code interaction with the Agent.\nL224: \nL225: This repository is the main entry point for ServingStudio, with compatible component revisions and shared commands to build and run them.\nL226: ## 🎬 Demo\nL227: \nL228: Explore the cite97†product introduction†syfi-servingstudio.github.io for workflows and performance case studies.\nL229: \nL230: ## 📣 News\nL231: \nL232:   * September 2026 · Introducing ServingStudio: a simulator, an Agent, and an interactive analysis application for LLM serving research and development.\nL233: ## 💡 Close the loop on serving performance\nL234: \nL235: ServingStudio connects simulation and implementation in one evidence-driven loop:\nL236: Step  | Stage  | What happens\nL237: --- | --- | ---\nL238: 1  | Set the target and baseline  | Define the model, request mix, hardware, and performance target, then measure the current deployment to establish a baseline.\nL239: 2  | Explore in simulation  | Sweep serving configurations and inspect predicted operation costs to select one change worth testing.\nL240: 3  | Implement one change  | Apply the selected change to vLLM or SGLang, from a kernel optimization to a new model implementation; pass correctness checks before measuring performance.\nL241: 4  | Profile the change  | Capture a GPU trace of the modified implementation and break down execution time across kernels, communication, and idle gaps.\nL242: 5  | Compare measurement with prediction  | Compare the measured breakdown with the Simulator's prediction; investigate whether each gap comes from the performance model, the implementation, or the measurement setup.\nL243: 6  | Validate end-to-end performance  | Measure throughput and latency on the full serving workload against the baseline; retain, reject, or refine the change, then feed the evidence into the next simulation pass.\nL244: The loop keeps the simulator grounded in measured execution while using its predictions to guide changes in the real system.\nL245: ## ✨ Key features\nL246: \nL247: ### 🧩 Flexible configuration\nL248: \nL249: Explore dense and mixture-of-experts models across precisions, GPU layouts, parallelism choices, and serving strategies.  |\nL250: \nL251: ### ⚡ Fast simulation\nL252: \nL253: Reach steady state and compare more configurations with a simulator designed to explore long workloads quickly.\nL254: \nL255: ### 🎯 Accurate predictions\nL256: \nL257: Ground predictions in kernel timings measured on real GPUs and calibrate them against vLLM and SGLang.  |\nL258: ### 🔎 Full observability\nL259: \nL260: Follow performance from the whole run through individual requests and scheduler steps down to model operations and kernels.\nL261: \nL262: ### 📊 Optimization insights\nL263: \nL264: Attribute simulated GPU time, compare it with the model's necessary work, and identify where performance can improve.  |\nL265: \nL266: ### 💬 Zero-code exploration\nL267: \nL268: Give the Agent a serving goal and receive experiments, analysis, tradeoffs, and supporting evidence without writing simulation code.\nL269: ## 🧩 The workspace\nL270: Component  | What it provides\nL271: --- | ---\nL272: cite103†ServingStudio Sim | Serving simulation, kernel profiling, framework alignment, and the ServingStudio Analyzer result service.\nL273: cite104†ServingStudio Agent | Experiment execution, persistent conversations and workspaces, Docker runners, and configurable model providers.\nL274: cite105†ServingStudio UI | Six analysis views and an integrated Agent page in one browser application.\nL275: cite106†ServingStudio Intro | The product introduction, workflows, and case studies.\nL276: The workspace pins an exact commit for each component. Shared build and service commands live in the cite92†justfile and cite107†scripts/ . All component repositories use `main`.\nL277: ## 🚀 Quick start\nL278: ### Requirements\nL279:   * Linux, Git, Python 3 with cite108†uv†docs.astral.sh , Rust stable, Node.js 22/npm, and cite109†just†just.systems .\nL280:   * A native C/C++ build toolchain, CMake, Ninja, pkg-config, Protocol Buffers compiler, mold, and tmux. See the cite110†host setup commands .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - zhengkid/Dream-RSI: The offical repo for \"Dream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds\" · GitHub (https://github.com/zhengkid/Dream-RSI)\nciteturn86view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/zhengkid/Dream-RSI\",\"lineno\":200}); Total lines: 288\nL99:     * AVAILABLE ADD-ONS\nL100:       * cite10†GitHub Advanced SecurityEnterprise-grade security features L101:       * cite57†Copilot for BusinessEnterprise-grade AI features L102:       * cite58†Premium SupportEnterprise-grade 24/7 support L103: \nL104:   * cite59†Pricing L105: \nL106: Search`/`\nL107: \nL108: cite1†Sign in L109: \nL110: cite60†Sign up L111: \nL112: Appearance settings\nL113: You signed in with another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You signed out in another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. You switched accounts on another tab or window. Reload to refresh your session. Dismiss alert\nL114: \nL115:   1. cite61†zhengkid L116:   2. cite62†Dream-RSI L117: ## Repository navigation\nL118: \nL119:   *   * cite62†Code L120:   * cite63†Issues3 (3) L121:   * cite64†Pull requests2 (2) L122:   * cite65†Actions L123:   * cite66†Projects L124:   * cite67†Security and quality L125:   * cite68†Insights L126: \nL127: More items\nL128: \nL129: cite69†Image: zhengkid†avatars.githubusercontent.com L130: \nL131: cite62†Dream-RSI L132: \nL133: Public\nL134: \nL135:   * cite70†Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\nL136:   * cite70†Fork131 (131) L137:   * cite70†Star1.5k (1.5k) You must be signed in to star a repository\nL138: ## About\nL139: \nL140: The offical repo for \"Dream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds\"\nL141: \nL142: ### Resources\nL143: \nL144: cite71†Readme L145: \nL146: Cite this repository\nL147: \nL148: cite72†Activity L149: \nL150: ### Stars\nL151: \nL152: 1.5k stars\nL153: \nL154: ### Watchers\nL155: \nL156: 56 watching\nL157: \nL158: ### Forks\nL159: \nL160: cite73†131 forks L161: \nL162: cite74†Report repository L163: \nL164: main\nL165: \nL166: cite75†Branches cite76†Tags L167: \nL168: [Input: Go to file]\nL169: \nL170: Go to file\nL171: \nL172: Code\nL173: \nL174: Open more actions menu\nL175: \nL176: ## Latest commit\nL177: \nL178: \nL179: \nL180: ## History\nL181: \nL182: 4 Commits\nL183: ## Folders and files\nL184: \nL185: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL186: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL187: cite77†assets | cite77†assets |    |\nL188: cite78†papers | cite78†papers |    |\nL189: cite79†.gitignore | cite79†.gitignore |    |\nL190: cite80†CITATION.cff | cite80†CITATION.cff |    |\nL191: cite81†README.md | cite81†README.md |    |\nL192: \nL193: ## Repository files navigation\nL194: \nL195:   *   * cite82†README L196: \nL197: More items\nL198: \nL199:        cite83†Image: Google DeepMind cite84†Image: University of Maryland, College Park cite85†Image: University of Virginia L200: # Dream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement\nL201: through Evolving Worlds\nL202: \nL203: cite86†Image: An agent must dream to recursively self-improve. History is the world it dreams in. L204: \nL205: Tong Zheng^{1,2}, Xidong Wu^{1✉}, Zheng Zhang^{1✉}, Zhankui He^{3}, Chaoyi Zhang^{1}, Benjamin Coleman^{3}, Ruoqiao Wei^{1}, Di Bai^{3}, Haolin Liu^{4}, Rui Liu^{2}, Xue Wang^{1}, Yue Zhuan^{1}, Wang-Cheng Kang^{3}, Renkai Xiang^{1}, Heng Huang^{2}, Xinwu Cheng^{1}, Yunsong Guo^{1}\nL206: ^{1}Google  ·  ^{2}University of Maryland, College Park  ·  ^{3}Google DeepMind  ·  ^{4}University of Virginia\nL207: \nL208: ^{✉} Corresponding authors\nL209: \nL210: * * *\nL211: \nL212: Note\nL213: \nL214: Code is being prepared for release. See cite87†Release plan .\nL215: cite88†Image: Algorithm engineering: 1.22x faster downstream runtime, 1.74x less discovery compute, 162x fewer calls than SimpleTES. Mathematical optimization: 2 of 3 tasks at or above the selected baseline. GPU kernel engineering: 4 of 4 kernels improved, 2.09x higher performance at an equal budget, 2.43x fewer generations at equal performance. Zero gradient steps on the coding agent. Versus Recursive Fixed Exploration unless a published system is named; algorithm engineering on Gemini-3.1-Pro. L216: ## News\nL217: \nL218:   * Sep 2026 — Preprint and project page released: cite89†paper (PDF) · cite90†dream-rsi.com†www.dream-rsi.com L219: ## Overview\nL220: Progress in recursive self-improvement hinges on effective exploration. As self-improvement targets get harder, discovery stretches over thousands of proposal–evaluation cycles, and poor exploration wastes substantial computation on ineffective search directions.\nL221: Managing and improving the exploration strategy is the bottleneck, and current systems face a dilemma: fixed strategies cannot adapt as search spaces scale, while optimizing the policy online means navigating a vast meta-search space under delayed and expensive feedback — assessing one exploration policy requires observing how it shapes an entire discovery process.\nL222: Our key insight is that accumulated discovery history can serve as a replay simulator over the realized search space. A completed discovery process already records a structured tree of past exploration decisions and their realized code-execution outcomes. An alternative policy can traverse that tree differently: different subsets of recorded branches, in different orders, with different parallel groupings and stopping decisions.\nL223: Because all outcomes are already saved, evaluating it requires only reading past records — no rerunning of the discovery agent or the evaluator. By analogy with model-based RL and world models, the history becomes a world the agent can dream in.\nL224: Dream-RSI closes a self-improvement loop at the exploration layer. A lightweight orchestration layer makes exploration explicit and programmable — branching, parallel exploration, stopping — while leaving the underlying coding agent unchanged. ❶ the current policy drives online discovery and logs its traces; ❷ the recorded trees are converted into a reusable simulator pool; ❸ candidate policies are evaluated and refined by dreaming over that pool, which returns immediate, low-cost off-policy feedback.\nL225: The improved policy is redeployed online, continuously expanding the pool.\nL226: cite91†Image: The Dream-RSI loop: online explore, construct replay simulator, dreaming-based policy improvement L227: \nL228: cite90†Method, results and interactive walkthrough → dream-rsi.com†www.dream-rsi.com L229: ## Highlights\nL230:   * History as replay simulator. We conceptualize completed discovery histories as replay simulators, which makes delayed exploration feedback reusable for efficient meta-exploration policy evaluation.\nL231:   * Meta-layer RSI loop. Dream-RSI continuously collects discovery histories through online exploration, constructs replay simulators from them to refine meta-exploration strategies via dreaming, and redeploys the upgraded policy online.\nL232:   * Empirical validation. Across algorithm engineering, mathematical optimization and GPU kernel engineering, Dream-RSI improves both discovery effectiveness and efficiency in several settings.\nL233: ## Release plan\nL234: \nL235: Item  | Status\nL236: --- | ---\nL237: Paper (PDF)  | ✅ Available\nL238: Project page & interactive demo  | ✅ cite90†dream-rsi.com†www.dream-rsi.com L239: arXiv posting  | 🔜 In progress\nL240: Discovered programs  | ⏳ Being prepared\nL241: Full codebase  | ⏳ Being prepared\nL242: Reproduction scripts  | ⏳ Being prepared\nL243: ## Citation\nL244: \nL245:     @article{zheng2026dreamrsi,\nL246:       title   = {Dream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds},\nL247:       author  = {Zheng, Tong and Wu, Xidong and Zhang, Zheng and He, Zhankui and\nL248:                  Zhang, Chaoyi and Coleman, Benjamin and Wei, Ruoqiao and Bai, Di and\nL249:                  Liu, Haolin and Liu, Rui and Wang, Xue and Zhuan, Yue and\nL250:                  Kang, Wang-Cheng and Xiang, Renkai and Huang, Heng and\nL251:                  Cheng, Xinwu and Guo, Yunsong},\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAReaL-RSI: Adaptive Scaling and Coordination of Recursive Self-Improvement (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18083v1)\nciteturn86view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.18083v1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 264\nL59: Towards this end, we introduce AReaL-RSI to organize RSI on two timescales (Figure cite40†1 ). Within a campaign (i.e., one execution iteration of AReaL-RSI on a specified task), a leader agent selects researcher harnesses suited to the task and provides evidence-driven guidance to persistent researcher agents. Conditional memory sharing links findings to their sources and tested conditions, limits proactive propagation of unresolved problems, and leaves recipients responsible for assessing applicability.\nL60: After a campaign, evidence-based review revises research orientation, reusable knowledge, and operational skills for subsequent campaigns. These mechanisms address the three difficulties through explicit coordination and revision procedures. Our contributions are summarized as follows:\nL61: Contribution 1. We formulate adaptive RSI scaling as a problem of organizing persistent investigations and reusable research procedures under a declared resource allowance (§cite7†2 ). The formulation distinguishes current artifact quality from investigative promise and separates the opportunity offered by additional computation from the efficiency of research team organization, which connects task-conditioned team composition and leader-agent guidance to adaptation within and across research campaigns.\nL62: Contribution 2. We develop conditional memory sharing (§cite11†3 ) and experience-driven researcher-harness evolution to support productive scaling (§cite15†4 ). Source-linked findings support selective propagation and recipient adaptation, while post-campaign review converts experimental experience into revisions of research orientation, knowledge, and skills. The mechanisms distinguish reuse among ongoing investigations from changes to the procedures available to future teams.\nL63: Contribution 3. We evaluate AReaL-RSI against Codex-Goal [cite41†14 ] across downstream harness optimization, GPU kernel optimization, and AI research tasks with two LLM backbones (§cite19†5 ). Under the reported resource allocations, AReaL-RSI increases Terminal Bench resolution by 2.2 percentage points for each backbone and achieves 32.2–36.8% higher mean KernelBench speedup.\nL64: Further comparisons of scaling research configurations support AReaL-RSI ’s coordinated scaling design: experience sharing with specialized researcher roles achieves 1.9–27.5% lower kernel latency than independent exploration and 1.3–33.9% lower latency than sharing alone across all seven comparable task–backbone pairs.\nL65: ## 2 Adaptive RSI Scaling\nL66: \nL67: Scaling RSI requires organizing additional computation into useful investigations. We define the scaling objective, then describe team composition and leader-agent guidance. This organization supports sharing within campaigns and harness revision across campaigns (Figure cite40†1 ).\nL68: ### 2.1 Why Scale Out RSI?\nL69: Parallel researcher agents can test competing hypotheses under costly or delayed feedback. However, duplicate work and indiscriminate sharing can waste computation or cause premature convergence. Effective scaling requires coordinating investigative priorities and information exchange alongside inference and experiment costs. Retaining useful procedures in the harnesses of a researcher agent creates a second opportunity: effort spent on one task can improve how later tasks are investigated.\nL70: Problem Formulation. A campaign is one execution of AReaL-RSI on a task $q_{n}$ with a persistent objective and a fixed incoming harness-library snapshot. Let $n$ index campaigns, $\\pi$ denote an admissible organization policy, and $x_{n}^{\\mathrm{out}}(\\pi)$ denote the output artifact. The final utility $U_{n}(x)$ is task-specific, with larger values preferred and invalid outputs assigned the task’s failure value.\nL71: For resource expenditure vector $\\mathbf{C}_{n}^{\\mathrm{total}}(\\pi)$ and allowance $\\mathbf{B}_{n}$, the objective is\nL72:  | $$\\max_{\\pi}\\;\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[U_{n}\\!\\left(x_{n}^{\\mathrm{out}}(\\pi)\\right)\\right]\\quad\\mathrm{subject\\ to}\\quad\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\mathbf{C}_{n}^{\\mathrm{total}}(\\pi)\\right]\\preceq\\mathbf{B}_{n}.$$  |  | (1)\nL73: The expectation covers stochastic research and evaluation; $\\preceq$ imposes componentwise resource constraints. The policy governs team selection, guidance, and sharing. Costs include researcher-agent execution, leader-agent inference, experiments, and post-campaign review (Equation cite42†13 ); review benefits are assessed in later campaigns. This per-campaign objective defines a comparison rather than a runtime optimizer.\nL74: ### 2.2 Task-Conditioned Team Composition\nL75: \nL76: Each campaign uses a fixed team of $K$ researcher agents. The task $q_{n}$ specifies the objective, admissible artifacts, constraints, and evaluation interface. Every researcher agent investigates the complete task and retains local state across interactions. Researcher harnesses provide complementary orientations, knowledge, and skills (§cite16†4.1 ).\nL77: Harness Selection. At campaign creation, the system snapshots the harness library as $\\overline{\\mathcal{L}}_{n}$. The leader agent selects harness assignments according to the task’s constraints, unresolved questions, and verification cost:\nL78: \nL79:  | $$(h_{n,1},\\ldots,h_{n,K})\\sim\\pi_{\\mathrm{select}}(\\,\\cdot\\mid q_{n},\\overline{\\mathcal{L}}_{n}),\\qquad h_{n,i}\\in\\overline{\\mathcal{L}}_{n}.$$  |  | (2)\nL80: Here $h_{n,i}$ is researcher agent $i$’s harness and $\\pi_{\\mathrm{select}}$ is the leader agent’s selection policy. Multiple researcher agents may receive the same harness when an orientation merits additional exploration. Assigned versions remain fixed within the campaign, although local working files remain editable. Guidance adjusts investigative focus without replacing team members or dividing responsibility into disjoint subtasks.\nL81: ### 2.3 Evidence-Driven Coordination\nL82: \nL83: The leader agent uses progress reports and experimental records to guide the team. At coordination event $t$, let $\\mathcal{E}_{n,t}$ denote the available public evidence and $g_{n,t}$ the leader agent’s context: inspected evidence, the current plan, and reported resource availability. Actions follow\nL84: \nL85:  | $$a^{\\mathrm{L}}_{n,t}\\sim\\pi_{\\mathrm{coord}}(\\,\\cdot\\mid q_{n},g_{n,t}),$$  |  | (3)\nL86: where $\\pi_{\\mathrm{coord}}$ uses model reasoning and tools to request evidence, revise the plan, advise a researcher agent, or await feedback.\nL87: \nL88: Quality vs Promise. Coordination distinguishes artifact quality from investigative promise. Let $\\mathcal{X}^{\\mathrm{ok}}_{n,t}$ contain saved, non-withdrawn candidates that passed the available checks, and let $s_{n}(x)$ be their recorded score, oriented so that larger values are better. For a nonempty candidate set, the incumbent is\nL89:  | $$x^{\\star}_{n,t}\\in\\operatorname*{arg\\,max}_{x\\in\\mathcal{X}^{\\mathrm{ok}}_{n,t}}s_{n}(x).$$  |  | (4)\nL90: The incumbent records observed quality under the performed checks, without establishing correctness on untested conditions or identifying the most promising direction. An implementation error may obscure a useful idea, while an early gain may depend on narrow conditions. The leader agent weighs explanations, unresolved tests, and remaining resources before recommending continuation, diagnosis, redirection, or integration.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDream-RSI: Recursive Self-Improvement through Evolving Worlds (https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14858v2)\nciteturn86view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2609.14858v2\",\"lineno\":90}); Total lines: 3011\nL84: To improve this policy, Dream-RSI follows a self-improvement loop (Figure cite79†1 ): (1) Online Exploration, where the current policy drives real-world discovery and logs execution traces; (2) Replay Simulator Construction, where recorded discovery trees become a reusable replay simulator pool; and (3) Dreaming-based Policy Improvement, where candidate policies are cheaply evaluated by dreaming over the simulator.\nL85: The updated policy is redeployed online, generating new experience that expands the simulator pool and closing an RSI loop at the meta-exploration layer. We evaluate Dream-RSI on 9 discovery tasks across four domains. On algorithm engineering (Lasso path solver), it outperforms sklearn and strong baselines while using up to $162\\times$ fewer agent calls than SimpleTES. On mathematical optimization, it matches or exceeds the best results among the compared baselines on two of three tasks.\nL86: On GPU kernel engineering (KernelBench), it reaches similar speeds with $1.79\\times$–$2.43\\times$ fewer generations, or improves kernel performance by up to $2.09\\times$ under a comparable budget. On harness optimization, it improves the seed harness by over 8 points on Terminal-Bench 2.1.\nL87: Our contributions are: 1) History as Replay Simulator. We recast completed discovery histories as replay simulators, making delayed exploration feedback reusable for cheap meta-exploration policy evaluation. 2) Meta-Layer RSI Loop. We introduce Dream-RSI, to our knowledge the first framework to close a meta-layer RSI loop using discovery history as a replay simulator. 3) Empirical Validation.\nL88: Across nine tasks in four domains, Dream-RSI achieves competitive discovery quality and improves discovery-agent-call efficiency in several settings.\nL89: cite80†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Discovery history as a replay simulator. A deployed policy first explores online, recording its execution traces as a discovery tree where each node is one attempt plus its full observation. This tree then acts as a simulator: a large number of candidate policies can be tested inside it, varying which branches to search, in what order, with how much concurrency, and when to stop.\nL90: Because every node’s outcome is already stored, one expensive online run can yield a large number of fast, low-cost off-policy evaluations. The agent can therefore \"dream\" over many alternative exploration strategies via historical replay, and redeploy only the improved policy.\nL91: ## 2 Motivation: Discovery History as a Replay Simulator\nL92: Consider an agent navigating an unfamiliar environment. On its first attempt it wanders, hits dead ends, and backtracks — but in doing so it builds a map. That map is reusable: a new policy can plan over it, skip known dead ends, revisit earlier choices, and compare routes without physically walking them again (cite81†Gupta et al., 2017 ). This is the logic of model-based reinforcement learning (cite82†Sutton, 1990 ; cite83†M. Moerland et al., 2023 ).\nL93: A model of how the environment responds to actions lets policies be trained or evaluated in simulation instead of through repeated real interaction (cite75†Ha and Schmidhuber, 2018 ). The Dreamer family (cite84†Hafner et al., 2019 ; cite85†Hafner et al., 2020 ; cite76†Hafner et al., 2023 ; cite86†Hafner et al., 2025 ) is the clearest example: learn a compact dynamics model from collected experience, then improve the policy by imagining trajectories inside it.\nL94: Long-horizon discovery has the same structure. An exploration policy decides which directions to pursue, which candidates to refine, what to branch on in parallel, and when to stop. Running it online leaves behind a structured history — branches explored, decisions made, costs incurred, outcomes observed. As shown in Figure cite77†2 , we treat this history as an empirical replay simulator: a grounded model of the part of the discovery space already seen.\nL95: Inside this simulator, a different exploration policy simply traces a different path through the recorded discovery tree, choosing other branches, another ordering, a different degree of parallelism, or an earlier stopping point. Scoring that path only requires revealing outcomes already stored along it; no rerun of the coding agent or evaluator is needed. One expensive online run therefore funds many cheap evaluations of alternative strategies.\nL96: ## 3 Dream-RSI: RSI through Evolving Worlds\nL97: As shown in Figure cite79†1 , Dream-RSI alternates between online exploration and offline “dreaming” for policy evaluation to improve the exploration policy that allocates discovery computation. For online rollout, the exploration policy directs a fixed discovery agent to generate a tree-structure discovery trace, where each node in the tree is an discovery attempt and is scored by a fixed evaluator.\nL98: The discovery tree then becomes a replay world: alternative policies can be re-evaluated against stored outcomes. In the offline dreaming phase, a fixed agent iteratively revises the code for the exploration policy, and every revision is evaluated in all stored replay worlds. The best version is then deployed for the next online rollout. Only the policy code changes; the models, evaluator, and execution interfaces stay fixed.\nL99: #### Discovery trees and the shared decision interface.\nL100: A discovery tree is rooted at $r$, the initial workspace state. Every non-root node $v$ has exactly one primary parent (root or a previous node), which decides where its attempt begins: the discovery agent resumes the parent’s saved workspace and inherits its accumulated observations as context to generate the attempt in $v$. Node $v$ keeps all previous generation history for it, and its own filesystem snapshot, together with an evaluation score $s_{v}$.\nL101: Scores are generated from a fixed protocol, and larger values are better. We define the root score $s_{r}=0$. In both online rollout and offline evaluation, the exploration policy observes a tree $\\mathcal{T}$, initially containing only the root, and selects nodes in $\\mathcal{T}$ to continue exploration. The eligible nodes are $A(\\mathcal{T})=\\{r\\}\\cup\\{v\\in\\mathcal{T}:v\\text{ is a leaf}\\}$, which contains the root and all leave node in the current tree.\nL102: We assume there are $W\\geq 1$ parallel workers, each running one generation–evaluation request at a time (e.g. concurrent API calls). For the current tree $\\mathcal{T}$, the exploration policy’s action is a batch $C\\in A(\\mathcal{T};W)$, where $A(\\mathcal{T};W)=\\{C\\subseteq A(\\mathcal{T}):|C|\\leq W\\}$ is the feasible batch set. The batch determines both where exploration continues and how many attempts run concurrently.\nL103: Both the online and offline phase use this decision interface, and differ only in how state transitions after an action: one executes for real, the other replays.\nL104: #### Online rollout.\nL105: Let $t=1,2,\\ldots$ index iterations of online rollout, starting from policy $\\pi_{1}$ and empty history $\\mathcal{H}_{0}=()$. At iteration $t$, the fixed policy $\\pi_{t}$ guides the discovery agent to generate a new online rollout with access to the completed discovery history $\\mathcal{H}_{t-1}$ as context. The history informs exploration but is kept separate from the tree under construction.\n"
